## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical knowledge of transactional memory in real workloads, careful fallback design, and low-latency queues.

## Key Concepts
- Intel TSX (_xbegin/_xend)
- CAS-based fallback paths
- Per-slot sequence counters to avoid ABA
- Cache-line alignment and false sharing avoidance
- Microbenchmarking for latency and throughput

## Code Example
```c
// Skeleton TSX-based enqueue
typedef struct { unsigned long long seq; unsigned long long val; } slot_t;
typedef struct { slot_t *slots; unsigned head, tail, cap; } q_t;
static inline int enqueue_htm(q_t *q, unsigned long long val) {
    unsigned pos = q->tail;
    if ((pos + 1) % q->cap == q->head) return -1; // full

    unsigned status = _xbegin();
    if (status == _XBEGIN_STARTED) {
        q->slots[pos].val = val;
        q->slots[pos].seq = pos + 1;
        q->tail = (pos + 1) % q->cap;
        _xend();
        return 0;
    }

    // Fallback: CAS-based enqueue with sequence validation
    return enqueue_cas(q, val);
}
```

## Design Considerations
- Retry limits: 3-5 TSX attempts before permanent fallback
- Alignment: 64-byte cache line boundaries
- Contention handling: exponential backoff in fallback path
- Benchmark: measure 99th percentile latency under 1 vs 16 producer/consumer pairs

## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates the ability to design crash-consistent data structures for persistent memory, requiring reasoning about durability guarantees, lock-free synchronization patterns, and recovery mechanisms after power loss.

## Key Concepts

- **Crash-consistency in PMEM**: Using CLWB (Cache Line Write Back) to flush modified cache lines to persistent media without invalidating, combined with SFENCE to enforce memory ordering and guarantee durability
- **Lock-free MPSC patterns**: Utilizing atomic operations on persistent indices to enable multiple producers to enqueue concurrently without locks
- **Recovery mechanisms**: Detecting and repairing partially written slots, reconstructing in-flight state after system restart

## Data Structures

```c
typedef struct {
    uint64_t seq;
    uint8_t payload[56];
} slot_t;

typedef struct {
    volatile uint64_t head;
    volatile uint64_t tail;
} pmem_queue_t;
```

Each slot is 64 bytes (cache-line aligned), with the sequence number serving as both a validity marker and a position indicator. The head and tail indices are stored in persistent memory to survive power failures.

