## Why This Is Asked
Tests mastery of crash‑consistency in non‑volatile memory, proper cache‑line flush semantics, and cross‑socket ordering. It probes how to design durable queues and recoveries with minimal latency impact.

## Key Concepts
- PMEM durability and flush/fence ordering (clwb/clflushopt, sfence)
- Release/acquire memory ordering and per‑slot sequencing
- Crash recovery and replay correctness in a bounded buffer

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo enqueue (high level, non‑blocking semantics)
function enqueue(slot, payload){
  slot.payload = payload; // write payload
  slot.len = payload.length;
  slot.seq = slot.seq + 1; // release semantics
  clwb(&slot.payload); clwb(&slot.len); clwb(&slot.seq);
  sfence();
}
```

```javascript
// Pseudo dequeue
function dequeue(){
  if (ring.head.seq == ring.tail.seq) return null; // empty
  let s = ring.head; let payload = s.payload;
  // advance head after acquire read
  ring.head = next(s);
  s.done = true; clwb(&s.done); sfence();
  return payload;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
