## Why This Is Asked
This question tests understanding of low-level memory management, per-thread caching strategies, lock-free data structures, memory ordering semantics, and NUMA awareness in practical allocator design.

## Key Concepts
- Fixed-size block allocators
- Thread-local storage (TLS) freelists with global slab pool
- Lock-free freelist with ABA avoidance using atomic compare-exchange
- Memory ordering: acquire/release semantics
- NUMA locality considerations and fragmentation

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-code: alloc/free for fixed-size blocks
typedef struct Block { struct Block* next; } Block;
Block* tls_freelist_head;
Block* global_pool_pull();

Block* alloc() {
  Block* b = tls_freelist_head;
  if (!b) {
    b = global_pool_pull();
    if (!b) return NULL; // OOM
  }
  tls_freelist_head = b->next;
  return b;
}

void free(Block* b) {
  b->next = tls_freelist_head;
  tls_freelist_head = b;
}
```

## Why This Question Is Asked

This question assesses the ability to design robust, low-latency IPC primitives under NUMA-aware multi-producer/multi-consumer contention with precise memory ordering requirements.

## Key Concepts

- Lock-free design, with ABA avoidance via per-slot sequence counters
- NUMA-aware memory placement to minimize cross-socket memory traffic
- Shadow consumer tail optimization to reduce remote cache-line access
- Cache-line alignment to prevent false sharing
- Memory ordering semantics (acquire/release, memory_order_acq_rel)

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct {
    volatile uint64_t sequence;
    char data[56];
} slot_t;

typedef struct {
    slot_t slots[N] __attribute__((aligned(64)));
    volatile uint64_t head;
    volatile uint64_t tail;
    volatile uint64_t shadow_tail;
} mpmc_queue_t;

static inline int enqueue(mpmc_queue_t* q, const void* data) {
    uint64_t slot = __atomic_fetch_add(&q->tail, 1, __ATOMIC_ACQ_REL);
    slot_t* s = &q->slots[slot & (N - 1)];
    while (s->sequence != slot) { /* spin */ }
    memcpy(s->data, data, 56);
    __atomic_store(&s->sequence, slot + 1, __ATOMIC_RELEASE);
    return 0;
}

static inline int dequeue(mpmc_queue_t* q, void* data) {
    uint64_t slot = __atomic_fetch_add(&q->head, 1, __ATOMIC_ACQ_REL);
    slot_t* s = &q->slots[slot & (N - 1)];
    while (s->sequence != slot + 1) { /* spin */ }
    memcpy(data, s->data, 56);
    __atomic_store(&s->sequence, slot + N, __ATOMIC_RELEASE);
    return 0;
}
```

## Microbenchmark Outline

To isolate NUMA effects under asymmetric producer/consumer loads:

1. **Setup**: Run producers on socket 0, consumers on socket 1; measure round-trip latency and throughput.
2. **Workload injection**: Vary producer/consumer ratios (e.g., 10:1, 1:10, 5:5) to create asymmetric contention.
3. **Metrics**: Capture remote memory accesses via `numactl --hardware`, cache-miss ratios via `perf stat`, and per-socket latency distributions.
4. **Validation**: Compare interleaved vs. local-only memory placement to quantify NUMA effects; verify shadow tail reduces remote cache-line bouncing.

## Why This Is Asked

Persistent memory requires crash-safe state transitions. This task tests ability to design data structures that survive power loss, with proper ordering and visibility guarantees across multiple producers and consumers.

## Key Concepts

- Persistent memory semantics and durable flush boundaries (clwb/psnc, SFENCE)
- Per-slot sequence counters to distinguish empty/full states
- CAS-based head/tail reservation and release semantics
- Epoch-based reclamation for safe memory reuse

## Code Example

```c
// Pseudo-enqueue (simplified)
bool pmq_enqueue(PMQ *q, void *payload) {
  uint64_t h = atomic_fetch_add_explicit(&q->head, 1, memory_order_relaxed);
  Slot *s = &q->slots[h & (N-1)];
  if (s->seq != h) return false;
  memcpy(s->payload, payload, PAYLOAD_SIZE);
  atomic_store_explicit(&s->seq, h+1, memory_order_release);
  // flush payload and seq to NVM, ensure visibility
  clwb((void*)s, PAYLOAD_SIZE);
  SFENCE();
  return true;
}

// Pseudo-dequeue (simplified)
bool pmq_dequeue(PMQ *q, void *out) {
  uint64_t t = atomic_load_explicit(&q->tail, memory_order_relaxed);
  Slot *s = &q->slots[t & (N-1)];
  if (s->seq != t+1) return false;
  memcpy(out, s->payload, PAYLOAD_SIZE);
  atomic_store_explicit(&s->seq, t+N+1, memory_order_release);
  atomic_fetch_add_explicit(&q->tail, 1, memory_order_relaxed);
  return true;
}
```

